# Batch 34 (2026-11-21): deep African and Asian prehistory and the Bronze/Iron Age Near East and steppe (Kabwe, Sibudu, Lion Cavern, Nataruk, Red Deer Cave, Calanais, Egtved, Mitanni, Midas, Croesus, Pazyryk, Linear B);
# the early medieval world 500-1000 CE and its legends; modern contactees, Nazi-occult myths and later folklore
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: deep prehistory and the Bronze/Iron Age
E+=[
X('kabwe-skull','Kabwe 1 (Broken Hill skull): the first archaic human fossil found in Africa',H,'confirmed',2,-299000,['archaeological','fossil','radiometric dating'],
 "Kabwe 1, also called Broken Hill Man or Rhodesian Man, is a nearly complete archaic human skull found on 17 June 1921 by the Swiss miner Tom Zwiglaar in the lead-zinc mine at Broken Hill, Northern Rhodesia (now Kabwe, Zambia). It was the first archaic human fossil discovered in Africa. The bones were sent to the British Museum, where Arthur Smith Woodward named a new species, Homo rhodesiensis; today the skull is usually classified as Homo heidelbergensis. The skull has a massive brow ridge and a long, low forehead, and shows severe tooth decay. A 2020 dating study put it at roughly 300,000 years old (about 324,000–274,000 years), which means it may have lived at the same time as early Homo sapiens and Homo naledi.",
 [('Which species?','Researchers disagree over whether Kabwe belongs to Homo heidelbergensis, a separate African species (H. rhodesiensis), or a population ancestral to modern humans; the younger date makes a direct ancestor role less likely and supports the picture of several human lineages living in Africa at the same time.'),
  ('Repatriation','Zambia has asked the United Kingdom to return the skull, which is held by the Natural History Museum in London; the request is part of wider debates about fossils and artefacts taken during the colonial period.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41586-020-2165-4','wiki:Kabwe_1'],img='Kabwe 1',dateNote='About 324,000–274,000 years ago (2020 dating); found 1921'),
X('sibudu-cave','Sibudu Cave: early arrows, glue and bedding in KwaZulu-Natal',H,'confirmed',2,-77000,['archaeological','luminescence dating'],
 "Sibudu Cave is a rock shelter in a sandstone cliff above the Tongati River, about 40 km north of Durban in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa. It was occupied, with gaps, between about 77,000 and 38,000 years ago, and its deposits are well preserved and dated by optically stimulated luminescence. Excavations led by Lyn Wadley of the University of the Witwatersrand since 1998 have found some of the earliest evidence of complex technology: stone points used as arrow tips around 64,000 years ago, a bone point interpreted as an arrowhead and a possible bone needle around 61,000 years ago, compound glues made by heating plant gum with ochre, and layers of sedge bedding from about 77,000 years ago. In 2024 the site became part of the World Heritage property 'The Emergence of Modern Humans: the Pleistocene occupation sites of South Africa'.",
 [('Modern minds?','Many researchers argue that making compound glue, which needs planning and control of heat, shows the same kind of thinking as modern humans; others caution that the link between particular technologies and cognitive abilities is hard to prove.'),
  ('Earliest bows?','The identification of the oldest stone and bone points as arrowheads rests on wear and damage patterns and residues; it is widely accepted but debated, and claims for the earliest bow use keep being revised as new sites are studied.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.jas.2007.11.006','wiki:Sibudu_Cave'],img='File:Sibudu cave KZN 02.JPG',end=-38000),
X('lion-cavern-ngwenya','Lion Cavern, Ngwenya: the world\'s oldest mine?',H,'disputed',2,-43000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "The Ngwenya Mine on Bomvu Ridge, north-west of Mbabane in Eswatini, contains a haematite deposit that was dug for red and specular ochre in the Middle Stone Age; the ancient workings are called Lion Cavern. Archaeological work in the late 1960s and early 1970s found stone tools in the old workings, and radiocarbon dating gave ages first of more than 20,000 years and later of about 41,000–43,000 years for the oldest mining, which would make it the oldest known mine in the world. The ochre may have been used for body decoration, ritual and, later, rock painting by the ancestors of the San. From around 400 CE, farming communities mined iron ore there, and from 1964 to 1977 an open-pit mine exported millions of tonnes of ore, destroying much of the original ridge.",
 [('How old?','The 41,000–43,000-year figure comes from radiocarbon dates obtained decades ago, near the limit of the method; it is widely quoted, but the dating has not been redone with modern techniques, so some researchers treat the exact age as uncertain.'),
  ('Ochre and meaning','Ochre use in southern Africa goes back far earlier, at sites such as Blombos Cave; why people invested so much effort in mining it, whether for pigment, hide-working, medicine or ritual, is still debated.')],
 ['wiki:Ngwenya_Mine'],img='Ngwenya Mine',dateNote='Oldest mining dated about 43,000–41,000 years ago (radiocarbon, 1960s–70s)'),
X('nataruk-massacre','Nataruk: a prehistoric massacre by Lake Turkana?',H,'disputed',2,-8000,['archaeological','bioarchaeological'],
 "At Nataruk, near Lake Turkana in Kenya, a team led by Marta Mirazón Lahr found the remains of at least 27 people dating to about 9,500–10,500 years ago, when the lake shore lay nearby. In a 2016 paper in Nature they reported that many, including a pregnant woman and children, showed signs of a violent death, with injuries from clubs and stone-tipped weapons, and two men had stone points lodged in the skull and chest. They interpreted the site as a massacre and the earliest scientifically dated evidence of conflict between groups of hunter-gatherers. The bodies appeared to have been left unburied in a lagoon.",
 [('Massacre','The excavators argue that the pattern of injuries and the lack of burial show one group being attacked by another, suggesting that warfare was not only a product of farming and settled life.'),
  ('A cemetery instead?','In a response in Nature, Christopher Stojanowski and colleagues argued that much of the damage could have happened after death, and that the articulated, orderly skeletons fit burial in a cemetery better than a massacre; the original team stands by its interpretation. Jebel Sahaba in Sudan, dated earlier, also shows repeated violence.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature16477','wiki:Nataruk'],img='File:2. Excavations at NATARUK.jpg',end=-7500,dateNote='About 10,500–9,500 years ago'),
X('red-deer-cave-people','The Red Deer Cave people: an archaic-looking population in Ice Age China',H,'disputed',3,-15800,['fossil','genetic','radiocarbon'],
 "The Red Deer Cave people are known from human bones found at Maludong (Red Deer Cave) in Yunnan and Longlin Cave in Guangxi, south-west China, dated to between about 17,800 and 11,500 years ago. The skulls and jaws show an unusual mix of archaic and modern features. When they were described in 2012, the researchers suggested they could represent a late-surviving archaic population or hybrids with an archaic group, and the media called them a possible new species. In 2022 a partial genome from a skull cap was published, suggesting they were modern humans related to people in East and Southeast Asia and the Americas. Large deer were cooked in the cave, which gave the people their name.",
 [('Archaic survivors','The original describers proposed that the unusual features might reflect a population of archaic humans or interbreeding with Denisovans or others surviving late in southern China.'),
  ('Robust modern humans','Other anthropologists argued from the start that the bones fall within the range of early modern humans; the 2022 genome supported this, although some researchers have questioned whether it was contaminated by modern DNA, and a 2025 response rejected that criticism.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0031918','wiki:Red_Deer_Cave_people'],img='Red Deer Cave people',end=-9500,dateNote='About 17,800–11,500 years ago'),
X('calanais-stones','The Calanais Stones: a cross-shaped circle and the petrified giants of Lewis',H,'confirmed',2,-2750,['archaeological','folklore'],
 "The Calanais (Callanish) Stones on the west coast of the Isle of Lewis in the Outer Hebrides of Scotland are a Neolithic stone circle with a tall central monolith and rows of standing stones running out from it, giving a roughly cross-shaped plan. They were set up around 2750 BCE and were used for ceremonies for many centuries in the Neolithic and Bronze Age; a small chambered tomb was later built inside the circle. Several other circles and settings nearby form the wider Calanais complex. The site is cared for by Historic Environment Scotland.",
 [('Lunar observatory?','Alexander Thom and Gerald Hawkins argued that the stones were aligned on the moon, and some researchers link them to the major lunar standstill, when the moon appears to skim the Clisham hills of Harris every 18.6 years. Critics note that some alignments occur by chance in any large arrangement of stones, and that weathering and movement make the original layout uncertain.'),
  ('Folklore','According to one local tradition the stones were giants turned to stone for refusing to become Christian; in the 17th century islanders called them fir bhrèige, "false men". Another story says a "Shining One" walks the avenue on midsummer morning, heralded by the cuckoo.')],
 ['wiki:Callanish_Stones'],img='Callanish Stones'),
X('egtved-girl','The Egtved Girl: a Bronze Age teenager and the debate over her travels',H,'confirmed',2,-1370,['archaeological','dendrochronology','isotopic'],
 "The Egtved Girl was a young woman of the Nordic Bronze Age, aged about 16 to 18, whose oak-log coffin was found in the Storehøj mound near Egtved in Jutland, Denmark, in 1921. Tree-ring dating places her burial in the summer of 1370 BCE. Her bones had dissolved, but her hair, teeth, nails, and some skin and brain tissue survived, together with her clothes: a woollen blouse and a short corded skirt, with a bronze belt plate decorated with spirals. A bark container held the remains of a fermented drink, and the cremated bones of a child of about five or six had been placed with her. The finds are in the National Museum of Denmark.",
 [('A traveller from afar?','A 2015 study of strontium isotopes in her hair, teeth and nails concluded that she was born outside present-day Denmark, perhaps in the Black Forest region of southern Germany, and travelled back and forth over long distances in her last two years, which was taken as evidence of long-distance alliances between chiefdoms.'),
  ('Or local after all?','Later researchers argued that the strontium signal could have come from farm lime or other local sources, and that she could have been local or from elsewhere in Scandinavia; the question remains open.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/srep10431','wiki:Egtved_Girl'],img='Egtved Girl'),
X('mitanni-indo-aryan-gods','Mitanni: Vedic gods in a Bronze Age treaty in Syria',H,'confirmed',2,-1550,['textual','linguistic'],
 "Mitanni (also called Hanigalbat or Naharin) was a Hurrian-speaking kingdom in northern Syria and south-eastern Anatolia from about 1550 to 1260 BCE, a great power alongside Egypt, the Hittites, Babylonia and Assyria. Its capital, Washukanni, has never been securely identified, and no royal annals have been found, so it is known mostly from its neighbours' records, including the Amarna letters. Unusually, its kings had Indo-Aryan throne names, a horse-training manual written by a Mitannian named Kikkuli uses Indo-Aryan technical terms, and a treaty of about the 14th century BCE between the Hittite king Suppiluliuma I and the Mitanni king Shattiwaza, found at Hattusa, calls on the gods Mitra, Varuna, Indra and the Nasatyas, deities also named in the Rigveda.",
 [('Who were the Indo-Aryans of Mitanni?','Most scholars think a small Indo-Aryan-speaking elite, perhaps linked with horse and chariot warfare, settled among the Hurrians and was absorbed, leaving names, gods and technical words behind; how and when they arrived, and how they relate to the peoples who brought Vedic culture into South Asia, is debated.'),
  ('Dating the Vedas','Because the treaty names Vedic gods, it is sometimes used as a fixed point in debates about when Vedic religion took shape; scholars disagree about how much it can tell us.')],
 ['wiki:Mitanni','wiki:Indo-Aryan_superstrate_in_Mitanni'],img='File:Carte du Mitanni-en.svg',end=-1260,dateNote='Kingdom c. 1550–1260 BCE; treaty 14th century BCE'),
X('gordion-midas','King Midas: the golden touch and the real king of Phrygia',M,'myth-legend',1,-740,['archaeological','textual','mythological','dendrochronology'],
 "In Greek myth King Midas of Phrygia was granted his wish that everything he touched would turn to gold, until even his food did, and he was cured by washing in the river Pactolus; in another story Apollo gave him donkey's ears. Behind the legend stands a real king: a Midas ruled Phrygia from its capital Gordion, near modern Ankara, in the late 8th century BCE, and is generally identified with Mita of Mushki, who appears in Assyrian texts between about 718 and 709 BCE. Herodotus says he was the first foreigner to send an offering to Delphi. At Gordion, Tumulus MM ('Midas Mound'), over 50 metres high, was built around 740 BCE according to tree-ring dates; excavated in 1957, it contained the body of an older man on purple and gold textiles in a log coffin with bronze vessels and furniture.",
 [('Midas or his father?','Because the tomb was built around 740 BCE, while Midas was still active in Assyrian records decades later, many archaeologists think the man in Tumulus MM was his predecessor, perhaps Gordias, rather than Midas himself.'),
  ('Myth and reality','Scholars suggest the golden-touch story may echo the wealth of Phrygia and of neighbouring Lydia, where the Pactolus carried gold-bearing sand; residues from the funeral feast vessels in Tumulus MM were analysed and found to come from a mixed drink of wine, beer and mead. Alexander the Great later cut the Gordian Knot at Gordion.')],
 ['wiki:Midas','wiki:Gordion'],img='File:Tumulus of King Midas 02.jpg',statusNote='Midas a real king; the golden touch is myth'),
X('croesus-lydian-coins','Croesus of Lydia: the first coins and "a great empire will be destroyed"',H,'confirmed',1,-630,['archaeological','numismatic','textual'],
 "Lydia, a kingdom in western Anatolia with its capital at Sardis, is credited with the world's first coins: lumps of electrum, a natural alloy of gold and silver found in the river Pactolus, stamped with a lion's head, in the 7th century BCE. Croesus, king from about 585 to 546 BCE, became proverbial for his wealth ('as rich as Croesus'); coins of pure gold and pure silver, the 'croeseids', are traditionally attributed to his reign. According to Herodotus, Croesus asked the oracle at Delphi whether to attack Persia and was told that if he did he would destroy a great empire; he attacked, and the empire destroyed was his own when Cyrus the Great took Sardis in about 546 BCE.",
 [('Croesus on the pyre','Herodotus tells how Croesus, about to be burned alive, called out the name of Solon, who had warned him that no one should be called happy until death, and was saved by a rainstorm sent by Apollo; an Athenian vase of about 500 BCE shows Croesus on a pyre. Historians treat the story as legend, and his real fate is unknown.'),
  ('Who made the first coin?','Some numismatists date the first electrum coins to the late 7th century BCE and note that Greek cities in Ionia struck early coins at about the same time; whether Lydians or Ionian Greeks came first, and whether the croeseids belong to Croesus or to the early Persian period, are debated.')],
 ['wiki:Croesus','wiki:Lydia','wiki:Croeseid'],img='File:British Museum gold coin of Croesus.jpg',end=-546),
X('pazyryk-burials','The Pazyryk burials: frozen tombs of the Altai horse-riders',H,'confirmed',2,-400,['archaeological','permafrost preservation'],
 "The Pazyryk burials are Scythian-type (Saka) kurgans of the 4th–3rd centuries BCE in the Pazyryk valley and on the Ukok plateau in the Altai Mountains of Siberia, near the borders of Russia, China, Kazakhstan and Mongolia. Water seeped into the timber burial chambers in antiquity and froze, so organic materials survived in the permafrost: tattooed bodies, felt hangings, Chinese silk, horses with elaborate trappings, wooden furniture and the Pazyryk carpet, the oldest known pile carpet. The main tombs were excavated by Sergei Rudenko from 1947, and the carpet is in the Hermitage Museum in St Petersburg. The site is part of the Golden Mountains of Altai World Heritage Site.",
 [('Who made the carpet?','Some specialists think the Pazyryk carpet was imported from Persia or Armenia, citing designs close to Achaemenid art; others think it was made locally by Altai nomads; the question is still argued.'),
  ('Tombs and tomb robbers','Most of the main kurgans had been robbed in antiquity, and the robbers\' tunnels let in the water that froze; ironically, the looting helped preserve what was left.')],
 ['wiki:Pazyryk_burials','wiki:Pazyryk_culture'],img='File:Pazyryk carpet.jpg',end=-200),
X('linear-b-decipherment','Linear B: the Bronze Age script that turned out to be Greek',H,'confirmed',2,-1450,['textual','linguistic'],
 "Linear B is a syllabic script used to write Mycenaean Greek, the earliest attested form of Greek, from about 1450 BCE until the collapse of the Mycenaean palaces around 1200 BCE. It was adapted from the earlier, still undeciphered Linear A of Minoan Crete. Thousands of clay tablets, mostly palace accounts of goods, livestock, land and workers, were found at Knossos, Pylos, Thebes, Mycenae and elsewhere; many survived only because the palaces burned and baked the clay. The script was deciphered in 1952 by the English architect Michael Ventris, building on the work of the American classicist Alice Kober, and the result was published with John Chadwick.",
 [('An unexpected answer','Before 1952 Arthur Evans and most scholars assumed the script recorded a non-Greek Minoan language; Ventris himself expected Etruscan. The discovery that it was Greek pushed the history of the Greek language back by centuries.'),
  ('Doubters','For several years a minority of scholars rejected the decipherment, until a tablet from Pylos with pictures of tripods and vessels was read as matching the Greek words for them; today the decipherment is universally accepted. Ventris died in a car crash in 1956.')],
 ['doi:10.2307/628239','wiki:Linear_B','wiki:Michael_Ventris'],img='Linear B',end=-1200,changed="Before 1952 Linear B was assumed to record an unknown Minoan language; Ventris' decipherment (1952) showed it was an early form of Greek, rewriting the early history of Greece."),
]
# --- part 2: the early medieval world and its legends; lost tribes and lost oases
E+=[
X('sri-maha-bodhi','Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi: a 2,300-year-old tree from the Buddha\'s fig',H,'confirmed',2,-236,['textual','botanical','oral tradition'],
 "The Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi is a sacred fig tree (Ficus religiosa) in the Mahamewuna Garden of Anuradhapura, the ancient royal capital of Sri Lanka. According to the Sri Lankan chronicle the Mahavamsa, it was grown from a cutting of the southern branch of the tree at Bodh Gaya in India under which the Buddha is believed to have attained enlightenment, brought to the island in 236 BCE (traditional date) by the nun Sanghamitta, a daughter of the emperor Ashoka, during the reign of King Devanampiya Tissa. It is described as the oldest living human-planted tree in the world with a recorded planting date. Anuradhapura, now a World Heritage Site, was the centre of Theravada Buddhism and of the island's great irrigation works for well over a thousand years.",
 [('Chronicle and tree','The planting date comes from the Mahavamsa, a Buddhist chronicle compiled in the 5th–6th centuries CE from older traditions; historians accept that the tree has been tended continuously for many centuries, but the exact early date rests on the chronicle.'),
  ('The original tree','The tree at Bodh Gaya itself has been destroyed and replanted several times, and saplings from the Sri Lankan tree are said to have been used to replace it; this makes the Anuradhapura tree, in Buddhist tradition, a link back to the original.')],
 ['wiki:Jaya_Sri_Maha_Bodhi','wiki:Anuradhapura'],img='Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi',dateNote='236 BCE (traditional date in the Mahavamsa)'),
X('baekje-incense-burner','The Gilt-bronze Incense Burner of Baekje: a lost kingdom\'s masterpiece found in the mud',H,'confirmed',2,567,['archaeological'],
 "In 1993 archaeologists excavating a temple site at Neungsan-ri in Buyeo, South Korea, the last capital of the kingdom of Baekje, found a large gilt-bronze incense burner preserved in a waterlogged pit. Standing 64 cm high and weighing 11.8 kg, it was probably made in the 6th century. Its lid rises as a mountain landscape full of figures, animals and musicians, crowned by a phoenix, and the bowl rests on a dragon. It was the largest of about 450 objects found at the site and was designated National Treasure of Korea No. 287 in 1996; it is displayed at the Buyeo National Museum. Baekje was conquered in 660 by an alliance of Silla and Tang China, and few of its works survived.",
 [('Hidden in haste?','Archaeologists have suggested the burner was buried deliberately to save it, perhaps when the Silla–Tang armies captured Buyeo in 660, which would explain why such a precious object lay in a workshop pit.'),
  ('Chinese model, Korean art','The design follows Chinese "boshan" mountain censers, which show the island mountains of the immortals; scholars debate how much its imagery reflects Daoist ideas of immortality, Buddhist cosmology or Baekje\'s own beliefs.')],
 ['wiki:Gilt-bronze_Incense_Burner_of_Baekje','wiki:Baekje'],img='File:Baekje Incense Burner NT 287 (1).jpg',dateNote='Probably 6th century; found 1993'),
X('wu-zetian','Wu Zetian: China\'s only female emperor and her unopened tomb',H,'confirmed',1,690,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Wu Zhao (624–705), known as Wu Zetian, was the only undisputed female sovereign in Chinese history. A former concubine of Emperor Taizong of Tang, she became empress to his son Gaozong in 655, governed for him after his stroke in 660, and ruled through her sons after his death in 683. In 690 she proclaimed her own Zhou dynasty and took the title of emperor, ruling until she was forced to abdicate in 705. She used informants and harsh laws, purged rivals, expanded the civil service examinations to promote officials by merit, and was a major patron of Buddhism. She was buried with Gaozong in the Qianling Mausoleum near Xi'an, which is believed never to have been looted and has not been excavated.",
 [('A wicked usurper?','Traditional Confucian historians portrayed her as a ruthless usurper who murdered her own children and rivals; some accusations, such as the killing of her infant daughter, are now regarded by many historians as later propaganda or impossible to verify.'),
  ('A capable ruler','Many modern historians emphasise her administrative ability, the stability of her reign and her promotion of talented officials, while still recognising the violence of her purges. A blank "wordless stele" stands at her tomb, variously interpreted as modesty or a challenge to later historians.')],
 ['wiki:Wu_Zetian','wiki:Qianling_Mausoleum'],img='Wu Zetian',end=705),
X('an-lushan-rebellion','The An Lushan rebellion: the war that broke the Tang dynasty',H,'confirmed',2,755,['textual'],
 "The An Lushan rebellion was a civil war in China from 755 to 763, at the midpoint of the Tang dynasty. An Lushan, a frontier general of Sogdian and Turkic descent who had become a favourite at the court of Emperor Xuanzong, launched a revolt from Fanyang (near modern Beijing) on 16 December 755 and declared a new Yan dynasty. The rebels captured Luoyang and then the capital Chang'an; the emperor fled towards Sichuan, and on the way his troops forced the execution of his beloved consort Yang Guifei, whose family they blamed. An Lushan was murdered by his son in 757, and the rebellion passed to Shi Siming and his son before collapsing in 763, with help for the Tang from Uyghur and other allied forces.",
 [('How many died?','Tang census returns fell from about 53 million people before the war to about 17 million afterwards, and the rebellion is sometimes listed among the deadliest wars in history. Most historians think much of the drop reflects the collapse of tax registration rather than deaths alone, so the true toll is unknown.'),
  ('A turning point','The war weakened central government, left regional military governors (jiedushi) powerful, and is often seen as the turning point of the Tang; it also inspired some of the greatest Chinese poetry, including works by Du Fu, and the later legend of Yang Guifei as a tragic beauty.')],
 ['wiki:An_Lushan_rebellion','wiki:An_Lushan','wiki:Yang_Guifei'],img='File:An Lushan Rebellion.png',end=763),
X('charlemagne','Charlemagne: crowned emperor in 800, and the king who sleeps in a mountain',H,'confirmed',1,800,['textual','archaeological','legend'],
 "Charlemagne (748–814) became king of the Franks in 768, conquered the Lombard kingdom of Italy in 774, and through decades of campaigns brought Saxony, Bavaria and much of western and central Europe under his rule, forcibly converting the Saxons. On Christmas Day 800, Pope Leo III crowned him emperor in St Peter's in Rome, the first emperor in the west in about three centuries. His court at Aachen became the centre of a revival of learning, the Carolingian Renaissance, which produced the clear Carolingian minuscule handwriting from which modern lower-case letters descend. He exchanged embassies with the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid, who sent him an elephant named Abul-Abbas. He died in 814 and was buried at Aachen.",
 [('The opened tomb','According to an 11th-century chronicle, Emperor Otto III opened Charlemagne\'s tomb in the year 1000 and found him seated upright on a throne, crowned and incorrupt; historians regard the details as legend.'),
  ('Sleeping hero','Like King Arthur and Frederick Barbarossa, Charlemagne appears in folk legend as a king asleep inside a mountain, such as the Untersberg near Salzburg, waiting to return in his people\'s hour of need; medieval epics such as the Song of Roland also made him a legendary figure.')],
 ['wiki:Charlemagne','wiki:Carolingian_Renaissance'],img='Charlemagne',end=814),
X('norse-greenland','The Norse of Greenland: a colony that vanished',H,'confirmed',2,985,['archaeological','textual','isotopic'],
 "Norse settlers from Iceland, led according to the sagas by Erik the Red, began settling south-west Greenland around 985–986. At its height the Eastern Settlement had perhaps 4,000 people, with about 500 farms and 16 churches, and the smaller Western Settlement lay further north. The colonists kept cattle and sheep, hunted seals, and exported walrus ivory to Europe. Greenland was also the base for voyages to Vinland in North America. The Western Settlement was abandoned around the mid-14th century, and the last written record from the Eastern Settlement is of a wedding at Hvalsey church in 1408. When a Danish-Norwegian mission arrived in 1721 to find the Norse, it found only Inuit, whose Thule ancestors had moved into Greenland from about 1200.",
 [('Why did they disappear?','Proposed explanations include the cooling of the Little Ice Age, the collapse of the European market for walrus ivory, isolation as ships from Norway became rare, conflict with the Inuit, soil erosion, and gradual emigration; most researchers now think several factors combined.'),
  ('Failed or adaptable?','Some writers, notably Jared Diamond, portrayed the Norse as too conservative to adapt; isotope studies of skeletons show they ate increasing amounts of seal and fish, and many archaeologists now argue they adapted considerably before leaving or dying out.')],
 ['wiki:Norse_Greenland','wiki:Eastern_Settlement','wiki:Hvalsey_Church'],img='File:Hvalsey Church.jpg',end=1450,dateNote='c. 985 – mid-15th century'),
X('huanchaquito-las-llamas','Huanchaquito-Las Llamas: the Chimú mass sacrifice of children and llamas',H,'confirmed',2,1450,['archaeological','bioarchaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "At Huanchaquito-Las Llamas, on the coast of northern Peru near the Chimú capital Chan Chan, archaeologists led by Gabriel Prieto and John Verano excavated in the 2010s the remains of more than 140 children, mostly aged between about 5 and 14, and over 200 young llamas, sacrificed in a single event dated to around 1450 CE. Many of the children's breastbones showed cuts consistent with their chests being opened, possibly to remove the heart. The burials lay in a layer of mud, which the excavators suggest was laid down after heavy rain and flooding on the normally arid coast.",
 [('A response to El Niño?','The excavators proposed that the sacrifice was an attempt to stop the extreme rains and floods of an El Niño event that would have devastated the Chimú economy; this is a widely discussed interpretation but cannot be proven directly.'),
  ('Largest known?','It has been described as the largest known single mass sacrifice of children in the Americas, and perhaps in the world; further sacrifice sites have since been reported nearby. The Chimú kingdom was conquered by the Inca around 1470.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0211691','wiki:Chimor'],img=None),
X('ten-lost-tribes','The Ten Lost Tribes of Israel: the exile that launched a thousand theories',M,'disputed',1,-720,['textual','archaeological','genetic','oral tradition'],
 "According to the Bible, ten of the twelve tribes of Israel lived in the northern Kingdom of Israel, which was conquered by the Neo-Assyrian Empire around 722–720 BCE; its people were deported and resettled elsewhere in the empire, as Assyrian records and the Bible both describe. Only Judah and Benjamin, in the southern kingdom of Judah, and part of Levi remained identifiable. The Jewish historian Josephus wrote in the 1st century that the ten tribes still lived beyond the Euphrates as an immense multitude, and in later Jewish and Christian tradition their return was linked with the coming of the Messiah. Over the centuries, groups from Afghanistan and India to Africa, Japan, the British Isles and the Americas have been claimed as their descendants.",
 [('Mainstream historians','Most historians think the Assyrians deported only part of Israel\'s population, mainly the elite, and that the deportees were gradually absorbed into other peoples, while many Israelites remained in the land or fled south to Judah; the Samaritans trace their descent to them.'),
  ('Descent claims','Claims of lost-tribe descent have been made for the Pashtuns, the Bnei Menashe of north-east India, the Beta Israel of Ethiopia, the Lemba of southern Africa, Native Americans, and the British (British Israelism), among others; a few communities have been recognised by Israeli religious authorities, while most claims are regarded by historians and geneticists as unsupported.')],
 ['wiki:Ten_Lost_Tribes','wiki:Assyrian_captivity_of_Israel'],img='Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III',statusNote='Assyrian conquest confirmed; fate of the tribes legendary'),
X('lemba-ngoma','The Lemba: a lost-tribe tradition, DNA and the "drum that thunders"',H,'disputed',2,1996,['genetic','oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "The Lemba (who call themselves Sena and are also known as Mwenye) are a people of South Africa, Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Malawi whose oral traditions say their ancestors came by sea from a city called Sena, and who kept customs such as food taboos, male circumcision and ritual slaughter that reminded outsiders of Jewish practice. Y-chromosome studies by Spurdle and Jenkins (1996), Thomas and colleagues (2000) and Himla Soodyall (2013) found that a large share of Lemba paternal ancestry comes from western Asia, while their maternal ancestry is African; the 2000 study reported the 'Cohen modal haplotype', associated with Jewish priestly families, among some Lemba men. Their tradition also tells of a sacred drum, the ngoma lungundu, said to have been the Ark of the Covenant.",
 [('Israelite or Arabian ancestry?','The 2000 study was widely reported as genetic support for Jewish ancestry. Soodyall\'s higher-resolution 2013 study did not detect Y chromosomes typically linked to Jewish ancestry and suggested the non-African ancestry more probably came from Arab and other traders of the Indian Ocean network; most researchers now think the ancestors of Sena were likely in southern Arabia, possibly the Hadhramaut in Yemen.'),
  ('The ngoma and the Ark','In 2007 the historian Tudor Parfitt identified an old ngoma in a museum in Harare and suggested in a 2008 book and documentary that it was a descendant of the biblical Ark; most scholars regard the link to the Ark as speculative.')],
 ['doi:10.1086/302749','wiki:Lemba_people'],img='Lemba people',dateNote='Genetic studies 1996–2013; date of the ancestral migration unknown'),
X('city-of-ys','Ys: the Breton city drowned by the sea',M,'myth-legend',2,1480,['mythological','folklore','textual'],
 "Ys (Kêr-Is in Breton) is a legendary city on the coast of Brittany, usually placed in the Bay of Douarnenez, which was swallowed by the sea. In the best-known version, King Gradlon ruled a rich city built on land reclaimed from the sea and protected by a dike whose gate had a single key. His wayward daughter Dahut stole the key, often at the urging of a lover who was the Devil in disguise, and opened the gate; the sea poured in, and as Gradlon fled on horseback a saint ordered him to throw off his daughter, who became a sea spirit. The story appears to have taken shape between the late 15th century, when Pierre Le Baud mentioned Ys in 1480, and the 17th century, with Dahut first named in 1680, and was popularised in the 19th century.",
 [('Memory of a drowned coast?','Some writers suggest the legend preserves a memory of the rising sea and of land and settlements lost on the Breton coast, as with Lyonesse and the drowned lands of Wales; there is no archaeological evidence for a city of Ys, and folklorists see the story as a moral tale of sin and divine punishment.'),
  ('Bells under the sea','19th-century folklorists recorded beliefs that the ruins could be seen at low tide or its church bells heard beneath the waves, a motif found in many drowned-city legends from Kitezh to Cardigan Bay.')],
 ['wiki:Ys','wiki:Gradlon'],img='Ys',dateNote='Legend set in the 5th–6th centuries; first written mention 1480'),
X('zerzura','Zerzura: the lost white oasis of the Sahara',M,'myth-legend',2,1250,['textual','exploration'],
 "Zerzura is a legendary oasis or city in the deserts west of the Nile. A village called Zarzura appears among abandoned villages in a 13th-century tax register of the Fayyum, and the treasure hunters' manual Kitab al-Kanuz describes a white city 'like a dove', with a bird carved on its gate holding a key in its beak, and a sleeping king and queen inside with great treasure. In 1835 the Egyptologist John Gardner Wilkinson reported an Arab account of an 'Oasis called Wadee Zerzoora' with palms, springs and ruins, west of the Farafra–Bahariya route. In the 1920s and 1930s explorers including Ralph Bagnold and László Almásy searched for it by car and plane; in 1932–33 Almásy and Patrick Clayton located rain-fed valleys in the Gilf Kebir plateau, sometimes identified as the 'Zerzura' wadis.",
 [('Real valleys','Some explorers argued that the Gilf Kebir valleys, with seasonal vegetation and rock art such as the Cave of Swimmers found by Almásy, were the real source of the legend; others think Zerzura was always a treasure-hunters\' fable.'),
  ('The Zerzura Club','Bagnold and his companions founded a light-hearted "Zerzura Club"; their desert experience later shaped the Long Range Desert Group in the Second World War, and Almásy\'s story inspired the novel and film The English Patient.')],
 ['wiki:Zerzura','wiki:Gilf_Kebir'],img='File:Gilf Kebir jan 2007 rock carvings.jpg',dateNote='First mention 13th century; searches 1830s–1930s'),
X('gumiho','The gumiho: Korea\'s nine-tailed fox',M,'myth-legend',3,1100,['mythological','folklore','textual'],
 "The gumiho or kumiho ('nine-tailed fox') is a fox spirit of Korean folklore, related to the Chinese jiuweihu, the Japanese kitsune and the Vietnamese hồ ly tinh. Korea's early histories, the Samguk sagi and Samguk yusa, already tell of white foxes able to take human form, but nine-tailed foxes appear in Korean records only from the Goryeo period onwards. In folk tales of the Joseon dynasty the gumiho usually appears as a deceptive and dangerous shapeshifter, often a beautiful woman, and in the well-known tale 'The Fox Sister' a much-wished-for daughter turns out to be a fox demon who secretly eats the family's livestock and then her parents. The term was also used to denounce treacherous officials.",
 [('Wicked or tragic?','Older Korean tales mostly show foxes as malevolent, which scholars partly link to Confucian views that placed animals below humans; in modern films and television dramas the gumiho has become a tragic figure who longs to become human.'),
  ('A shared East Asian creature','Folklorists trace the nine-tailed fox to ancient Chinese texts such as the Shanhaijing, where it could be an omen of good fortune; its image in Korea, Japan and Vietnam developed differently in each culture.')],
 ['wiki:Gumiho','wiki:Nine-tailed_fox'],img='Gumiho',dateNote='Goryeo period onwards (fox spirits earlier)'),
]
# --- part 3: spirits and demons, UFO religions and contactees, Nazi-occult myths, modern legends and mysteries
E+=[
X('adze-firefly-vampire','The adze: the Ewe firefly vampire',M,'myth-legend',3,1900,['folklore','oral tradition'],
 "The adze is a vampiric being in the folklore of the Ewe people of Togo and Ghana. In the wild it takes the form of a firefly, slipping through keyholes, cracks in walls or under doors at night to suck the blood of sleepers, who fall sick and die; if captured it turns into a human shape. It can also possess people, who are then regarded as witches. Traditional accounts describe suspicion falling on people thought to have reason for envy: women whose brothers' children were doing better than their own, old people if the young were dying, or the poor who might envy the rich.",
 [('Malaria in folk form?','Folklorists and writers have suggested that stories of a small flying night creature that sucks blood and causes fatal illness may reflect experience of mosquitoes and malaria; this is an interpretation, not something the tradition itself states.'),
  ('Witchcraft and envy','Anthropologists see the adze, like many witchcraft beliefs, as a way of explaining misfortune through social tensions such as jealousy within families, which could lead to accusations against vulnerable people.')],
 ['wiki:Adze_(folklore)','wiki:Ewe_people'],img=None,dateNote='Recorded in 20th-century ethnography; age of the belief unknown'),
X('rakshasa','Rakshasas: the shape-shifting demons of the Ramayana and Mahabharata',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['mythological','textual'],
 "Rakshasas are a race of usually malevolent beings in Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions, and in Indonesian folk belief; the female form is rakshasi. They live on Earth but have supernatural powers, which they use to disrupt Vedic sacrifices, take on illusory forms or eat humans. The most famous is Ravana, the ten-headed rakshasa king of Lanka, who abducts Sita in the Ramayana. In the Mahabharata, the hero Bhima kills the cannibal rakshasa Hidimba and marries his sister Hidimbi; their son Ghatotkacha fights for the Pandavas. Not all rakshasas are evil: Ravana's brother Vibhishana sides with Rama.",
 [('Mythic enemies or memory of real peoples?','Some 19th- and 20th-century writers argued that rakshasas were a demonised memory of real non-Vedic peoples of the forests or the south; most modern scholars regard this as speculative and treat rakshasas as figures of religious imagination.'),
  ('Across Asia','Rakshasas spread with Hindu and Buddhist art and texts to South-east and East Asia, appearing as guardian or demon figures in art and texts as far as Japan, where they are known as rasetsu.')],
 ['wiki:Rakshasa','wiki:Ravana'],img='File:Ravana British Museum.jpg',dateNote='In the epics, composed c. 5th century BCE – 4th century CE'),
X('sodder-children','The Sodder children: five who vanished in a Christmas fire (1945)',H,'disputed',2,1945,['documentary','forensic','testimony'],
 "Early on Christmas morning 1945 a fire destroyed the house of George and Jennie Sodder in Fayetteville, West Virginia. George, Jennie and four of the nine children at home escaped, but five children, aged between 5 and 14, were not seen again. The ladder normally kept by the house could not be found, the telephone did not work, and the fire brigade did not arrive until the morning. No remains were found in the ashes at the time, and the fire was officially blamed on faulty wiring. In 1949 a search supervised by a pathologist found a few vertebrae, which a Smithsonian specialist identified as coming from one person aged about 16–22, showing no sign of burning and probably mixed in with fill soil. The family put up a billboard with the children's photos that stood until after Jennie's death in 1989.",
 [('The family\'s view','The Sodders believed the children had been kidnapped, possibly by people connected to the Sicilian Mafia in retaliation for George\'s criticism of Mussolini, and pointed to the missing ladder, a cut telephone line and the lack of remains; they followed up claimed sightings for decades.'),
  ('The fire explanation','Investigators and many later commentators think the children most likely died in the fire, noting that a man admitted cutting the phone line while stealing equipment and that remains can be lost in a fierce fire and its debris; the case remains officially unresolved.')],
 ['wiki:Sodder_children_disappearance'],img=None),
X('billy-meier','Billy Meier and the Plejaren: the most photographed contactee',H,'debunked',2,1975,['photographic','testimony'],
 "Eduard 'Billy' Meier (born 1937) is a Swiss farmer who claims to have been in contact with human-like extraterrestrials from the Pleiades, whom he calls the Plejaren, since childhood, with contacts resuming in January 1975 with a being named Semjase. From the mid-1970s he produced hundreds of colour photographs and films of 'beamships' over the Swiss countryside, along with metal samples, sound recordings and long channelled messages, and he founded a group known as FIGU. His photos were widely published and became some of the most famous UFO images of the 1970s.",
 [('Hoax','Sceptics and many ufologists concluded that the photos were faked using small models. In 1997 Meier\'s ex-wife Kalliope said the spaceships were models he built from household objects such as trash-can lids, and that photos of the alien women "Asket" and "Nera" were really of Michelle DellaFave and Susan Lund, members of a television dance troupe. His messages have also been criticised for antisemitic content.'),
  ('Believers','Supporters argue that a one-armed farmer could not have faked so many images and films, and continue to promote his prophecies; there is no independent evidence for the Plejaren.')],
 ['wiki:Billy_Meier'],img='Billy Meier',statusNote='Photos widely judged to be models'),
X('raelism','Raël and the Elohim: the volcano encounter that founded a UFO religion',H,'confirmed',2,1973,['testimony','textual'],
 "Raëlism is a new religious movement founded by the French sports journalist Claude Vorilhon, who took the name Raël. In his 1974 book he wrote that on 13 December 1973, while walking in the crater of the Puy de Lassolas volcano near Clermont-Ferrand, he met a being from a spacecraft who told him over six days that humanity had been created in laboratories by extraterrestrials, the Elohim, mistaken in the Bible for God. Raëlism teaches that prophets including the Buddha, Jesus and Muhammad were sent by the Elohim, with Raël as the last, and that they will return when humanity builds them an embassy. In 2002 Clonaid, a company linked to the movement, announced the birth of a cloned baby, but no evidence was ever provided.",
 [('Religion or fiction?','Scholars of religion classify Raëlism as a UFO religion and study it as an atheistic reinterpretation of the Bible in the light of science and the space age; there is no independent evidence for Raël\'s encounter.'),
  ('Ancient astronauts','Raël\'s reading of the Elohim as alien scientists closely parallels the ancient-astronaut ideas popularised by Erich von Däniken a few years earlier, and the movement is part of the wider modern myth of gods as visitors from space.')],
 ['wiki:Raëlism','wiki:Raël'],img='File:Puy de Lassolas und Puy de la Vache.jpg',statusNote='The movement is real; the alien contact is an unverified claim'),
X('heavens-gate-1997','Heaven\'s Gate: the comet, the "Next Level" and 39 deaths (1997)',H,'confirmed',2,1997,['documentary','forensic'],
 "Heaven's Gate was an American new religious movement founded in 1974 by Marshall Applewhite and Bonnie Nettles, known as Do and Ti, who taught that followers could leave their human bodies and become immortal beings of 'the Evolutionary Level Above Human', reached by spacecraft. The group combined Christian millenarianism, New Age ideas and ufology, and ran a web-design business called Higher Source. Rumours spread that a spacecraft was travelling behind the bright comet Hale–Bopp. On 26 March 1997 sheriff's deputies found the bodies of all 39 active members, including Applewhite, in a house in Rancho Santa Fe, California; they had died in a coordinated series of ritual suicides timed with the comet's closest approach.",
 [('The comet rumour','In late 1996 an amateur astronomer\'s photograph appeared to show a "companion object" beside Hale–Bopp, and the claim was discussed on the radio show Coast to Coast AM; astronomers identified the object as a background star, but the group came to believe the comet signalled their departure.'),
  ('How to understand it','Scholars of new religions describe Heaven\'s Gate as a UFO religion and debate how isolation, charismatic leadership and the death of Nettles in 1985 reshaped its beliefs from bodily ascent to leaving the body behind.')],
 ['wiki:Heaven\'s_Gate_(religious_group)','wiki:Comet_Hale–Bopp'],img='File:Comet Hale-Bopp from State College PA 1997.jpg',end=None,dateNote='Group founded 1974; deaths March 1997'),
X('aetherius-society','The Aetherius Society: "the voice of Interplanetary Parliament" (1954)',H,'confirmed',3,1954,['testimony','textual'],
 "The Aetherius Society is a new religious movement founded in London in the mid-1950s by George King, who said that on 8 May 1954, in his flat in Maida Vale, a voice told him: 'Prepare yourself! You are to become the voice of Interplanetary Parliament.' King claimed to channel messages from 'Cosmic Masters' on other planets, and his followers combined Theosophy, yoga, spiritual healing and UFO beliefs. In 'Operation Starlight' (1958–1961) King and members climbed eighteen mountains around the world, including Holdstone Down in Devon, which they believe were charged with spiritual energy. The society still exists, with members mainly in Britain, the United States and New Zealand.",
 [('Scooped by a UFO?','In 1958 the society\'s magazine claimed the Cosmic Masters had told of an atomic accident in Russia; when the Kyshtym nuclear disaster of 1957 became known in the West in 1976, New Scientist ran a short item titled "Scooped by a UFO!". Sceptics note that vague claims of disasters can be matched to later events.'),
  ('A quiet UFO religion','Scholars describe the Aetherius Society as one of the earliest UFO religions and as relatively uncontroversial compared with others; there is no independent evidence for its extraterrestrial contacts.')],
 ['wiki:Aetherius_Society','wiki:George_King_(religious_leader)'],img='File:Holdstone Hill (Holdstone Down) - geograph.org.uk - 3275355.jpg'),
X('vril-coming-race','Vril: a Victorian novel that became a Nazi-occult myth',H,'debunked',2,1871,['textual'],
 "The Coming Race (1871), published anonymously by the British politician and novelist Edward Bulwer-Lytton, describes a narrator who descends into a mine and finds the Vril-ya, a superior race living in caverns, descended from people who fled underground to escape a great flood and who command an all-powerful energy called vril. The book was a popular sensation, and the meat extract Bovril took the second half of its name from it. Some occultists, notably Helena Blavatsky and Rudolf Steiner, treated vril as based on real occult knowledge. In 1960 Louis Pauwels and Jacques Bergier, in The Morning of the Magicians, claimed that a secret 'Vril Society' had existed in Weimar Berlin.",
 [('The Vril Society myth','Later esoteric and neo-Nazi writers expanded the story into a secret society of mediums who channelled aliens and built flying saucers for the Nazis; historians have found no evidence that such a society or its saucers existed, and trace the story to postwar fiction and speculation.'),
  ('Hollow earths and master races','The novel belongs to a wider 19th-century fascination with hidden underground worlds, alongside the hollow-earth ideas of Symmes and later legends of Agartha, and its theme of a superior race was later adopted by occult and racist movements.')],
 ['wiki:Vril','wiki:Bovril','wiki:The_Morning_of_the_Magicians'],img='Vril',statusNote='The novel is real; the Vril Society is unsupported by evidence'),
X('die-glocke','Die Glocke: the "Nazi Bell" that appeared in 2000',H,'debunked',2,2000,['textual','speculative'],
 "Die Glocke ('the Bell') is an alleged secret Nazi wonder weapon first described in detail by the Polish author Igor Witkowski in his book Prawda o Wunderwaffe ('The Truth About the Wonder Weapon', 2000) and popularised by the British journalist Nick Cook in The Hunt for Zero Point (2001). It was said to be a bell-shaped device about 4 metres high with counter-rotating cylinders filled with a radioactive substance called 'Xerum 525', tested in Lower Silesia, with effects variously described as anti-gravity, time travel or free energy. Witkowski said his information came from a transcript of the interrogation of SS general Jakob Sporrenberg, shown to him by an unnamed Polish official.",
 [('Recycled rumour','Mainstream reviewers describe the claims as pseudoscientific and based on unverifiable sources, noting that no documents, witnesses or physical remains have been independently confirmed; a concrete ring structure near Ludwikowice in Poland, called "the Henge" or "Flytrap" by proponents and claimed as a test rig for the Bell, has no documented link to any such device.'),
  ('Wonder-weapon legends','The Bell joined older postwar legends of Nazi flying saucers and secret technology, and has become a staple of fiction, television documentaries and video games.')],
 ['wiki:Die_Glocke_(conspiracy_theory)'],img=None),
X('akakor-tatunca-nara','Akakor: the lost underground city invented by a German "chieftain"',H,'debunked',2,1976,['testimony','investigative journalism'],
 "Akakor is an alleged ancient underground city somewhere between Brazil, Bolivia and Peru, described by the German journalist Karl Brugger in The Chronicle of Akakor (1976), based entirely on interviews with a man calling himself Tatunca Nara, chief of an Amazonian tribe. In the late 1980s Germany's Federal Criminal Police Office confirmed that Tatunca Nara was Günther Hauck, born in Coburg, Bavaria, in 1941, who had left Germany in the 1960s; he denies this. In 1990 the adventurer Rüdiger Nehberg and the producer Wolfgang Brög exposed him in a television documentary. Brugger was shot dead on Ipanema beach in Rio de Janeiro in 1984, and his killing is unsolved.",
 [('Disappearances','Three clients who went into the jungle with Tatunca Nara as a guide, from the United States in 1980, Switzerland in 1983 and Sweden in 1987, disappeared; remains identified as the Swiss traveller were later found. No charges linking him to the disappearances have been proven.'),
  ('A lasting legend','Despite the exposure, Akakor continues to appear in books on lost civilisations, and elements of the story were used in the film Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of the Crystal Skull (2008).')],
 ['wiki:Akakor','wiki:Tatunca_Nara','wiki:Karl_Brugger'],img=None),
X('kuchisake-onna','Kuchisake-onna: the slit-mouthed woman panic in Japan (1979)',M,'myth-legend',2,1979,['folklore','media reports'],
 "Kuchisake-onna ('slit-mouthed woman') is a figure of Japanese urban legend: a tall woman wearing a surgical mask who stops children and asks, 'Am I beautiful?' If they say yes, she removes the mask to reveal a mouth slit from ear to ear and asks again, and the wrong answer leads to violence; some versions say answering that she looks 'average' lets you escape. The modern legend appears to have started in Gifu Prefecture in 1978 and spread nationwide in 1979 after newspapers and magazines reported it, including the Gifu Nichi Nichi Shinbun in January 1979, leading some schools and parent groups to have children walk home in groups accompanied by adults.",
 [('Old yokai or modern rumour?','Some folklorists link her to older ghost stories of the Edo period about vengeful women spirits (onryō), while the literature scholar Iikura Yoshiyuki argues the legend is a product of the 1970s; the 1979 panic is well documented.'),
  ('A pattern of school rumours','The legend spread mainly among schoolchildren, like the "Hanako-san" toilet ghost and other modern Japanese school legends; she has since inspired many films, manga and games.')],
 ['wiki:Kuchisake-onna'],img='Kuchisake-onna',dateNote='Modern legend 1978–79; possible Edo-period roots'),
X('abiku-ogbanje','Abiku and ogbanje: West African children "born to die"',M,'myth-legend',3,1600,['oral tradition','ethnographic','literary'],
 "In Yoruba belief an abiku ('one born to die') is a spirit child who is born, dies young and returns to be born again to the same mother, causing repeated grief; the Igbo have a similar concept, the ọgbanje ('children who come and go'), whose spirit is tied to a hidden stone, the iyi-uwa, which must be found and destroyed to break the cycle. Families might give such children names begging them to stay, or mark their bodies so that a returning child could be recognised. A 2004 study of 1,695 women in Ondo and Ekiti states of Nigeria found more than half said they believed in abiku children. The idea appears in literature by Wole Soyinka, J. P. Clark, Chinua Achebe and Ben Okri, whose novel The Famished Road (1991) is narrated by an abiku.",
 [('Explaining child deaths','Researchers have suggested that the belief helped families make sense of repeated deaths of infants in times of high child mortality, and that some cases may reflect inherited conditions such as sickle cell disease; this is an interpretation of the belief, not part of it.'),
  ('Changelings elsewhere','Ogbanje is often compared with the changelings of European folklore, another tradition that explained sickly or unusual children as beings from the spirit world.')],
 ['wiki:Abiku','wiki:Ogbanje'],img=None,dateNote='Traditional belief of unknown age; recorded in literature since the 20th century'),
X('curupira','The Curupira: Brazil\'s backward-footed guardian of the forest',M,'myth-legend',3,1560,['folklore','textual'],
 "The Curupira is a forest spirit of Tupi-Guarani tradition in Brazil, Paraguay and the Guianas, a guardian of the forest who punishes hunters and loggers who take too much. It is usually described as a small man with his feet turned backwards, so that anyone following his tracks goes the wrong way, and in modern images he often has flaming red hair, perhaps through confusion with the similar Caipora. The first written mention is in a letter by the Jesuit José de Anchieta at São Vicente, São Paulo, dated 30 May 1560, which describes the Curupira as a feared being that attacked people and was appeased with offerings.",
 [('From demon to trickster','Under European influence the Curupira was gradually recast from a feared demon into a mischievous trickster who could be outwitted, though still capable of causing misfortune and death.'),
  ('A modern symbol','In Brazil the Curupira has become a popular symbol of forest protection and appears in children\'s books and on television; the legend is also found in Venezuela, Guyana, Peru and Paraguay, and may have passed from Carib-speaking to Guarani-speaking peoples.')],
 ['wiki:Curupira'],img='Curupira',dateNote='First written mention 1560'),
X('dulce-base','Dulce Base: a secret alien base under a New Mexico mesa?',H,'debunked',2,1979,['testimony','documentary'],
 "Dulce Base is the subject of a conspiracy theory that a joint human-alien underground facility lies beneath Archuleta Mesa near the town of Dulce, New Mexico, on the Jicarilla Apache reservation. The claims began with Paul Bennewitz, an Albuquerque businessman who from 1979 became convinced he was intercepting electronic signals from alien spacecraft near Kirtland Air Force Base. Influenced by the Air Force Office of Special Investigations agent Richard Doty, who fed him false information, Bennewitz came to believe he had discovered a base near Dulce populated by grey aliens and humans; by 1983 his claims were appearing in the popular press and they were later expanded by other writers into stories of underground battles and genetic experiments.",
 [('Disinformation','Researchers including Greg Bishop, in Project Beta (2005), argue that Bennewitz was deliberately fed disinformation to divert his attention from classified military projects at Kirtland, and that the pressure contributed to the breakdown of his mental health; in 1989 the ufologist William Moore said he had helped feed Bennewitz false information about aliens, and Moore and Doty are described as responsible for the disinformation campaign.'),
  ('An old story underground','The Dulce legend has been compared to the 1940s Shaver Mystery, with its evil subterranean beings flying disc-shaped craft; no physical evidence for a base has ever been found.')],
 ['wiki:Dulce_Base','wiki:Paul_Bennewitz'],img=None),
]
